A||N||8||rockcrawl||3800/4T60E axles from CV Unlimited||||1||
Z||000000||rockcrawl||03-27-2001||10:07 PM||||Last Thursday evening I called CV Unlimited and ordered axles for the 3800SC swap. I requested '94 Pontiac TranSport 3800/4T60E inner tripod joints and shafts with outer joints for a '87 Fiero 5spd. He asked what they were going to be used for and I told him. They shipped them out on Friday and I got them today (Tuesday). Very good service especially considering they had to assemble them just for me. Cost was $100 per axle and $15 freight.<BR> I installed them right away and found that the left one was too long. Here's what it looked like. notice the left inner boot is very compressed and the right inner boot is stretched.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/axlesbefore.jpg"> <BR>So I pulled them back out and compared them side by side. The left axle (female tripod) was longer by about 1/2". The left axle <B>should</B> be the shorter of the two.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/axles.jpg"> <BR> So I removed the clamps and swapped the tripods. Reinstalled the axles and this is what it looks like. It still seems like the left should be shorter and the right longer, but there is plenty of movement in and out. <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/axlesafter.jpg"> <P>I believe they must have made a mistake, or otherwise they were just confused about the application (mysterious rear engine vehicle). Anyway, I'm happy with the final results. Thanks terryk for the help finding the correct parts.<P>A question for anyone with a 4T60E in a Fiero. What is the difference in axle length between sides?<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||03-27-2001||10:38 PM||||Hmm, they did my first set.<P>The drivers side is stock A/U body. The passenger side is 4T60E A/U body. The 4T60E uses a shorter axle on that side than a 440/4T60. Sounds like they got confused.<P>I finally reduced it to this:<P>Order 94 Olds Ciera 3800 4T60E axles with 86 Fiero manual trans outer CV's. That nails it down.<P>They are great in creating whatever combination you want.||||reg||1
Z||000002||pherder||03-27-2001||11:27 PM||||Do they have the lengths, diameters, # of splines listed anywhere?<P>It would help some of us who are trying to do weird things like move the transaxle to the left 2" AND put in 2" wider (on each side) suspension pieces for a kit car project.  <P>Yes that means I can use a stock axle on the left but the right will be 4" longer than stock!<P>I emailed them a question about sizes but have gotten nothing back.  (And, No, I have not called them ... I prefer e-mail.)<P>Paul<P>------------------<BR>88 Formula<BR>88 Coupe<BR>86 SE<BR>95 GMC<BR>92 Mazda<BR>55 Jones<BR>Sedgwick, KS 67135<BR>pherder@kscable.com||||reg||5
Z||000003||Archie||03-28-2001||08:53 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pherder:<BR><B><BR>Yes that means I can use a stock axle on the left but the right will be 4" longer than stock!<P>Paul<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey Paul, is this on a stick shift Fiero?<P>If it is a stick shift car you're working on, then you'll find that GM does not make a FWD axle that is longer than the stock Fiero right side axle.<BR> <BR>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000004||Will||03-28-2001||12:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pherder:<BR><B>Do they have the lengths, diameters, # of splines listed anywhere?<P>It would help some of us who are trying to do weird things like move the transaxle to the left 2" AND put in 2" wider (on each side) suspension pieces for a kit car project.  <P>Yes that means I can use a stock axle on the left but the right will be 4" longer than stock!<P>I emailed them a question about sizes but have gotten nothing back.  (And, No, I have not called them ... I prefer e-mail.)<P>Paul<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You might try the extension shaft from a Beretta if you're using a stick shift. That will shorten the right axle, possible making it easier to come up with one.||||reg||1
Z||000005||rockcrawl||03-28-2001||04:58 PM||||I had found an axle rebuilder website that showed the dimensions of all the different axles but now I can't find it. They showed the same part # for a '94 Ciera 4spd and a '94 Silhouette 4spd.||||reg||1
Z||000006||pherder||03-28-2001||07:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR> Originally posted by Archie:  <BR><B>Hey Paul, is this on a stick shift Fiero?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes, but since you didn't respond to my email, I have been searching other places.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B> You might try the extension shaft from a Beretta if you're using a stick shift. That will shorten the right axle, possible making it easier to come up with one.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes, exactly what I was looking into see the thread in the mall ...<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum4/HTML/002815.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum4/HTML/002815.html</A> <P>That way there <B>MAY</B> be a stock axle that would work.<P>Otherwise I still would only need <B>ONE</B> custom axle made.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR> Originally posted by rockcrawl:  <BR><B>I had found an axle rebuilder website that showed the dimensions of all the different axles but now I can't find it. They showed the same part # for a '94 Ciera 4spd and a '94 Silhouette 4spd.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>ARRRGGGGHHH!!!!  That is what always happens to me ... find the site once ... never find it again.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>BTW, rockcrawl, if you DO find a source of intermediate shafts from 5 speed Berettas, I hope you share!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Paul<P>------------------<BR>88 Formula<BR>88 Coupe<BR>86 SE<BR>95 GMC<BR>92 Mazda<BR>55 Jones<BR>Sedgwick, KS 67135<BR>pherder@kscable.com||||reg||1
Z||000007||pherder||03-28-2001||08:03 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pherder:<BR><B>I emailed them a question about sizes but have gotten nothing back.  (And, No, I have not called them ... I prefer e-mail.)<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I got this from them (via e-mail)<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by paula (CVunlimited):<BR><B>Dear Paul<BR>Please give us a call at 1-800-868-0057 and ask for George or Franklin.<BR>They can answer any questions you may have<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I know, I know ... call them.  I had hoped an answer to at least one of my questions.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Paul||||reg||1
Z||000008||rockcrawl||03-28-2001||08:51 PM||||CV Unlimited never answered my email. I called and went through about 3 people before I got the right person. Not sure his name but he sounded like he knew what he was doing. He started talking about measuring this and that, but I convinced him that I had it all figured out and what I was telling him would work. He asked if the trans would be turned around in the Fiero or if it was just "slid to the back". I said it's just slid to the back. I think then he made an honest mistake and assembled the axles wrong. Still not sure, but no problem, I'm happy.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> The placement of my engine/trans indicates that the right axle should be 1.5" longer than the left. It is only 1/2" longer. The side cover is about 3/4" away from the cradle. So if the TranSport and the Fiero do have the same track width, the engine in the TranSport must be mounted 1" farther to the right (passenger side).<P>Paul, I'm planning the same thing for my Rossa. I'm going to build longer control arms for the rear (about 3"), so I need the intermediate shaft. I got the Beretta getrag but no shaft. There was someone on the forum that had one a few months ago for $75 but I don't remember who. I think that's too much anyway.<P>Here's the reply I got today:<BR>Dear Jon<BR>Please give us a call at 1-800-868-0057 and ask for George or Franklin.<BR>They can answer any question you may have.<P>Jon<p>[This message has been edited by rockcrawl (edited 03-29-2001).]||||reg||1
